  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nBroad upper troughing looks to persist through\nthe weekend and into next week, with temperatures returning to \nseasonal values for early October. The cooler temperatures will \nbe reinforced by a shortwave digging through the upper trough on\nSunday. This wave could pull moisture, from the boundary that \nbecomes diffuse to the south of our region after crossing today,\nnorthward, leading to some possible showers south and east of \nPittsburgh. At this time the probability of more than a few \nlight showers/sprinkles is fairly low (Prob > 0.05 inches around\n30% in the WV ridges). After Sunday, building surface high \npressure looks to keep the region dry for much of the rest of \nthe week. \n\nOne thing to watch will be the potential for frost both Tuesday and \nWednesday mornings to the northeast of Pittsburgh, as well as in the \nusual Laurel Highland/northern West Virginia cold spots. The NBM \npaints a 50% to 75% chance of low temperatures colder than 37 \ndegrees north of US-422 both mornings - which would be chilly enough \nfor at least patchy frost given clear and calm overnight conditions. \nTemperatures from Wednesday on should build back towards more \nseasonal values.",
